{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/104656-instinct-alternate-werewolf-vices","id":104656,"title":"Instinct: alternate werewolf Vices","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-02T23:50:24-07:00","id":104656,"title":"Instinct: alternate werewolf Vices","content":"I submitted this for the writer all-call, and wanted to share it with you guys to get feedback. The idea is that werewolves, torn between Wolf and Man, must balance conflicting natures. The Man carries over the Virtues, things he or she still believes, but Vice gives way to the predator Instinct. I'm realizing it's not as clear in this later draft, but the archetypes were meant to convey certain animal hunting techniques, or defensive or dominance maneuvers in large herbivores (the Juggernaut, for example, was meant to evoke a charging rhino).<br /> <br /> Anyway, I was pretty excited by the construction, and thought I'd share. Let me know what you think!<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Instinct</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">From the moment of the First Change, the Uratha live to hunt. Werewolves may maintain their human convictions into their new lives, but the predatory spirit driving them demands a bloody toll. The instincts gnawing at their heads push them to savage acts the laws of men condemn and the bans of spirits condone. Caught between these extremes, the Uratha hold their own to the Oath of the Moon and the bans of the Firstborn.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">When a human undergoes the First Change, he loses his Vice. In its place arises the Instinct. While old addictions and petulant rebellions offer familiar comforts, the Instinct stands him up against the crushing press of his duties. Even when his human half lives up to his highest Virtue, the heart of a predator still pounds against his ribcage. Instincts grant multiple reward conditions for regaining Willpower. Minor adherence to Instinct grants a single point's recovery, and triumphs refill the character's pool completely.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The following archetypes are presented as samples, but an inspired player or Storyteller should feel empowered to create their own. Beneath each description is a short list of deeds that reward the player for following the archetype. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Berserker</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Berserker overcomes her obstacles through brute force, frontal assaults, and sheer doggedness. No wound or setback gives her cause to retreat.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">: Confronting an opponent needlessly.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Risking death or grievous injury when a peaceful solution is possible.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Cannibal</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Cannibal takes whatever he needs from whatever is available, whether or not he has right on his side. He can be a bully, a thief, or a parasite to his packmates, but his lack of sentiment allows him to rip through to the heart of the matter.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Destroy something of yours or a packmate's to achieve your goal.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Commit an atrocity to bring your prey to ground.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Chaser</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Chaser lives for the pursuit. If her prey runs, she runs, and if her prey hides, she searches. Catching her quarry pales to the thrill of the hunt, and she will let her target rest when the kill is assured.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Follow a person or animal who tries to run or hide from you.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Watch your quarry regain its strength or hide its trail.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Catch or kill your quarry when you are both exhausted (when your Willpower points or empty health boxes are depleted).</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Juggernaut</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Only one force can stop the Juggernaut, and that is herself. She will breach any wall and shrug off any injury, and crush beneath her heel any who try to oppose her.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Foil another's attempt to manipulate you or to inflict lethal or aggravated damage on you.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Survive an attempt on your life.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Lurker</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Lurker watches, and waits. He watches his prey scan for threats. He waits for it to feel secure. He strikes when its guard falls.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower: </b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Learn an important detail about your prey's life.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower: </b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Kill your prey while he has every resource at his disposal.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Queen</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Queen brings multiple parts to bear against her prey. Her allies and her hunting grounds conspire to surround her target so no escape is possible. Whether she lands the killing blow or her ally, glory goes to the Queen.</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Cut off an opponent's escape.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Help an ally succeed at a goal they've shared with you.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Overcome with help something you could not have done yourself.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Scavenger</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">When others hunt, the Scavenger loots and steals. The dead offer up their flesh already, and the unwary leave their trophies unguarded. No glory lies in theft, but no risk either.</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower: </b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Take something that belongs to someone else.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Keep something that you stole from someone who tried to take it back.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Spider</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Let the wolves hunt, the Spider weaves such beautiful webs. To her, the snare must be tested, and perfected, and tested again. Whatever gets caught makes a fine meal, but the web is what sustains her.</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Ensnare a person in your schemes.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Ambush your prey, using a signature lure, camouflage, or blind.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Ambush a second prey using the exact same tactics and lure as before.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Stalker</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">No one can hide from the Stalker. He can smell spirits in human flesh as readily as a pack will track a herd of deer through a forest. Every new scent or lead draws the Stalker closer to the end of the hunt</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Discover a lead on the whereabouts of the person you seek.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Follow a trail of leads to its end.<br /> </span></span><br /> <b>Tempter</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Tempter takes his prey by giving it what it wants. If the promise of a gift can dispel generations of survival instinct, then why waste breath and time chasing the quarry?</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Convince someone to accept a gift or favor from you that they say they don't want.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Open a character's last Door with an average impression in Social Maneuvering.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Kill your target while they hold something you gave them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>Toolmaker</b><br /><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Toolmaker refuses to accept the limits of his body when hunting. Standing athwart two worlds puts the best of both within reach. Combining bloodlust with innovation perfects the hunter, and no prey can hope to survive.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Kill your prey with a weapon or a trap, while in a non-human form.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Kill a creature using a weapon or a trap that could not easily be brought down using the natural or supernatural gifts at your pack's disposal.</span></span> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>LFP: Foreign Bodies (Deviant: the Renegades)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","name":"Verge","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-03T05:53:27-07:00","id":104780,"title":"","content":"I have only one quick note regarding word count. I wouldn't have reproduced the &quot;<b>Single Willpower</b>&quot; or &quot;<b>All Willpower</b>&quot; whenever I add another example, just separate them by full stops, for example:<br /> <br /> <b>Tempter</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">The Tempter takes his prey by giving it what it wants. If the promise of a gift can dispel generations of survival instinct, then why waste breath and time chasing the quarry?</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>Single Willpower:</b> Convince someone to accept a gift or favor from you that they say they don't want.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"><b>All Willpower:</b></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Open a character's last Door with an average impression in Social Maneuvering.</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\"> Kill your target while they hold something you gave them.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-03T12:19:45-07:00","id":104999,"title":"","content":"I think my two issues with it are:<br /> <br /> 1) It seems to fall down on the basic premise of turn Virtue/Vice into a way to focus on Man vs. Wolf (esp. with Harmony being Flesh vs. Spirit, I like the idea of putting this dichotomy here). Mostly because the Instincts aren't really... wolfish. They all just feel like a cluster of normal Vices around themes of violence and social manipulation.<br /> <br /> 2) Adding in a All Willpower option makes Virtues kinda pointless. Where is the struggle for balance here? There's no incentive to follow your Virtue because the main bonus it gets you is now something your Instinct can get you as well. Why try to maintain your human side when your wolf side is mechanically better with no distinct drawback for doing so? In Requiem, Masks and Dirges are both capable of providing singe or all; so they reinforce the validity of trying to balance your Anchors. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-03T12:22:40-07:00","id":105002,"title":"","content":"Also, why the negative connotation equating instincts to vices?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-03T17:49:50-07:00","id":105231,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/104656-instinct-alternate-werewolf-vices?p=104999#post104999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think my two issues with it are:<br /> <br /> 1) It seems to fall down on the basic premise of turn Virtue/Vice into a way to focus on Man vs. Wolf (esp. with Harmony being Flesh vs. Spirit, I like the idea of putting this dichotomy here). Mostly because the Instincts aren't really... wolfish. They all just feel like a cluster of normal Vices around themes of violence and social manipulation.<br /> <br /> 2) Adding in a All Willpower option makes Virtues kinda pointless. Where is the struggle for balance here? There's no incentive to follow your Virtue because the main bonus it gets you is now something your Instinct can get you as well. Why try to maintain your human side when your wolf side is mechanically better with no distinct drawback for doing so? In Requiem, Masks and Dirges are both capable of providing singe or all; so they reinforce the validity of trying to balance your Anchors.</div> </div> </div> </div>1) This was actually written before the new Harmony rules were previewed, so there is a bit of thematic overlap, I suppose. And the problem with keeping it &quot;wolfish&quot; is that wolves don't stray far from pack hunting, scavenging, and hunting small prey. To keep it expansive, I included more hunting styles, as well as the fighting styles of large herd animals&quot;honored prey&quot;). The setting justification for this, again, lost in the short edit, was that Father Wolf, the Firstborn, and the thousands of generations unto the modern day have all consumed a variety of animal spirits, and can manifest any nature compatible with Rage. The game justification is that players like options.<br /> <br /> And they <i>are </i>a cluster of Vices around themes of violence and social manipulation. And hunting, and victimization. They're meant to capture the prey instinct of a werewolf. Normal Vices could substitute, but they almost seem silly in the face of Death Rage and a cultural imperative to kill the spirit-tainted. As an added bonus, they could easily be fulfilled <i>during</i> Death Rage, in a way that Vices could not.<br /> <br /> 2) Point taken. I would argue that Instincts are basically murder-drives; there's plenty of reasons not to indulge them in polite company.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Also, why the negative connotation equating instincts to vices?</span></span></div> </div> </div> </div> They're both id-level drives that one sinks to, rather than ideals like Virtue, or nature/demeanor archetypes like Mask/Dirge. Vices sate some base drive that is natural for social primates, and any negative connotations they may have are cultural, not objective. Instincts are similar, but they're coupled to a predator spirit, and have a culturally acceptable outlet (&quot;The Wolf must hunt.&quot;). Virtues remain in the pair, because werewolves retain reasoning faculties (most days), uphold creeds and oaths, and are ties back to the mortal world. Instinct and Virtue are also as diametrically opposed as Vice and Virtue, so the shoe fits.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>LFP: Foreign Bodies (Deviant: the Renegades)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","name":"Verge","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-03T21:14:16-07:00","id":105358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verge</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/104656-instinct-alternate-werewolf-vices?p=105231#post105231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) This was actually written before the new Harmony rules were previewed, so there is a bit of thematic overlap, I suppose.</div> </div> </div> </div>No no... I meant I like the general idea of moving the Man vs. Wolf aspect of the game to this set of mechanics. The new Harmony rules are more focused on Flesh vs Spirit, and some people are worried that the wolf side of the equation gets lost as Flesh tends to be written towards Man.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And the problem with keeping it &quot;wolfish&quot; is that wolves don't stray far from pack hunting, scavenging, and hunting small prey. </div> </div> </div>I think that's... a bit oversimplified. That's what wolves spend most of their time doing; it doesn't really represent the range of social instinctive roles wolves fall into within a pack.<br /> <br /> And since we're not dealing with real wolves, we can look at this on a more symbolic/human interpretive point of view.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T00:17:48-07:00","id":105420,"title":"","content":"Ah, sorry, I misread your objections.<br /> <br /> I'm not clear on your first item then: Instinct falls down on the wolf v man premise because the archetypes aren't wolfish enough? That's fair, but then you agree that there's room for symbolic, human interpretations. Do you mean human interpretations of wolf behavior, human symbolism surrounding the wolf, or something else?<br /> <br /> I aimed for something broader, but that still carried a sense of the predatory. With the Firstborn and Father Wolf, I felt the actual wolfskin was provided, and that Instinct could be used to broaden a player's options in how to play a werewolf, while underlining its key themes. These archetypes were written with the Uratha experience in mind, so that a player felt rewarded when thinking and acting like a slavering death machine, but wasn't compelled to carry the wolf's baggage any further than being a &quot;werewolf&quot; implies. Also, and this wasn't the intent, but the current set could be used for Changing Breeds.<br /> <br /> I want to know what you think would make this more successful in that wolf v man strain. I think there's plenty of room for development in the idea.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>LFP: Foreign Bodies (Deviant: the Renegades)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/969-verge","name":"Verge","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T02:05:48-07:00","id":105448,"title":"","content":"I think the overall issue is that your given Instincts... aren't really instincts. They're not even really animalistic. That's why it feels odds as a counterpoint to Virtues as representing Man. They don't really address the Man vs. Wolf (or other animal) mental divide. When I read the list the impression I get is that werewolves are all just sociopaths with different ways of enjoying killing shit, not werewolves are humans struggling with animal instincts that are now pushing at them to act in ways they didn't before the change. In other words I think you're taking the &quot;slavering death machine&quot; things too far here so that it comes out more like Slashers than wolves.<br /> <br /> To get to your first question though, all of the above. The werewolves here are human/wolf-spirit hybrids. They're not bonded to actual wolves, but spirits that are tied to wolf archetypes. Just as spirits aren't confined to a single type of representation, Instincts don't have to be either. Some instincts can be more mythological, others more modern general human perceptions, and others more accurate wolf instinctive drives. Each werewolf's spirit-wolf side gets to be expressed in a potentially unique way.<br /> <br /> In terms of capturing the feel more successfully, I would suggest two things:<br /> <br /> First they need a sense of immediacy. An Instinct isn't a quirk, inclination, or preference. It's something hard-wired into you. It is something that's triggered. Your list are generally method based. They're how a character approaches a problem; often proactively. If you want to get a real instinctive feel they should be reactive. That gut reaction is something for the Man to struggle against. The Berserker werewolf doesn't go into fights inherently of the attitude that when they fight, they don't stop until one side is dead. The Berserker werewolf gets challenged over something they cannot let go, and their instinct kicks in screaming in their head to fight with everything they have. Maybe they give in and go berserk on the opponent, or maybe the Man side reels it in to try de-escalate the situation instead.<br /> <br /> Second is to try to root them more in animalistic base motivators. Fight and flight are obvious; both there's more to wolves than that. Dedication to the survival of the pack (either immediate in a defensive sense, or long term regarding breeding and pup-rearing), pack functionality, etc. are all other basic urges the Wolf is pushing for. And there's a lot of interesting things you can do here. Consider as an example that female wolves will go into a false pregnancy instinctively when there's a true pregnancy in the pack; ensuring that only one litter is born and cared for at a time. Transplanting that on a werewolf can lead to some interesting character drama. Imagine a female werewolf who, thanks to her human brain having a much larger social unit than a wolf pack, going into sympathetic pregnancy mode every time a family member of close friend gets pregnant (possible before anyone even knows, and regardless of if the pregnancy will result in a live birth). Think of all the different ways different characters would handle having to deal with that. Do they go loner and avoid people so their wolf instincts to fall in line with the pack doesn't result in massive hormone releases they do not want to have to deal with? Do they try to balance things by purposefully associating with humans that aren't likely to kick that instinct off? Do they just go with it and deal with the physical issues because their friends and family are way too important to give up on?<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that particular example is a good one for an RPG, but I hope you can see where I'm going with it. If you combine what drives wolf behavior with triggers that you can try to avoid but once set off, you've got to deal with the consequences, you get both a more Man vs. Wolf feel of struggling with new/different instincts that don't mesh with the human only instincts the werewolf used to be stuck with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T12:28:29-07:00","id":105742,"title":"","content":"Claire Redfield already covered this previously in some prior musings/postings some time back; is this derived from that at all? I'm already using Reason/Instinct for the Virtue/Vice replacement in my own overhaul/house ruleset for a Chronicle myself with some ongoing tinkering. I think it's a good direction, but I don't think Instinct neatly replaces Vice more than Virtue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1LPTOgwgXBVVXxeyQKJOebzMgsN5vUl09k9fwLQVfNaw/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf: the Forsaken Overhaul</a>: A W:tF Shard and House Rules, Pre-Idigam Chronicles and taking different route. Constantly being Updated.<br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1CSomGcKor9qHrEb7nH15g29587CSzWQY5IgjCRRhdSI/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">New Bloodlines and Blood and Smoke Updates</a>: Homebrew Bloodlines and updates to prior ones.<br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nHsr9MBiPUrvZLsGDgwnEeEVsxxHFgBqJMUxcCw3BYk/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Belial's Brood Blood and Smoke</a>: An updated take on the Forsworn.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/114-tatterdemalion-king","name":"Tatterdemalion King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar114_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"241"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-08T02:03:55-07:00","id":108045,"title":"","content":"I think &quot;Reason / Instinct&quot; works really well; it just feels *wrong* for any template to have a Virtue without a Vice. I'm not sure that Instinct should really be reduced to the &quot;renegade&quot; option, either. Balance (Harmony, you could say) is what it's all about. Now I'm off to find these posts you mention. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance OPP. <b>Dark Eras</b>, <b>Shattered Dreams</b>, and counting!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2653-nated","name":"NateD","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"144"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}